ABSTRACT_EN: str = (
    'Background: Earthquake emergency information is stage-aware and time-sensitive. '
    'General-purpose LLMs remain limited in factual alignment and dynamic updating, '
    'while a single static knowledge base or retrieval path can hardly satisfy both verifiability and colloquial coverage. '
    'Objective: Focusing on Research on Earthquake Emergency Question Answering via Dynamic–Static Knowledge Collaboration, '
    'this thesis proposes a method that collaborates a knowledge graph, dense retrieval, and real-time seismic feeds under unified scheduling. '
    'Main work: (1) Static knowledge construction, including emergency topics and historical earthquake records, '
    'a graph schema of events–regions–topics–steps, and complementary topic-level vector retrieval. '
    '(2) Dynamic–static collaboration via CEIC speed reports and USGS catalogs, with pre-/during-/post-event phase priors '
    'and rule-threshold scheduling driven by confidence, availability, and urgency, injecting graph and retrieval evidence in partitioned prompts. '
    '(3) Generation and system realization with a LoRA-adapted model, quality guarding, failure degradation, '
    'and an end-to-end classify–probe–schedule–retrieve–assemble–generate pipeline deployable on Web and mobile demos. '
    '(4) Evaluation on a 60-question stratified set with baselines B0–B3 under offline ablation that disables dynamic feeds and output enrichment. '
    'Results: B3 factual consistency reaches 82.5%, higher than B0 (52.4%), B1 (66.8%), and B2 (41.2%); '
    'completeness means are 3.35 (B3) and 4.02 (B0), showing that stronger factual alignment does not equal uniformly higher coverage. '
    'Conclusion: The proposed method improves factual consistency under interpretable scheduling and yields a reproducible methodological baseline.'
)

